title: NuGet Fake-Job-Assessment Sideload - osk.exe Started From a User-Writable Path
id: 2dd18dd5-4940-4b1f-ad84-382a8b3d56f8
status: experimental
description: Detects the final stage of the 13 malicious NuGet packages reported by Socket in November 2026, where a build analyzer stages OskSupport.dll into %LOCALAPPDATA%\osk\ next to a copied osk.exe and a later button control starts that copy with a hidden window. The Windows on-screen keyboard only runs from System32, so an osk.exe executing from a user profile or temp directory is the campaign's sideload marker.
references:
  - https://socket.dev/blog/malicious-nuget-packages-fake-job-assessments
  - https://corgea.com/research/winui-nuget-fake-job-assessment-msbuild-osk-sideload-november-2026
  - https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/13-malicious-nuget-packages-turn-fake.html
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/26
tags:
  - attack.t1195
  - attack.t1036
  - attack.t1105
logsource:
  category: process_creation
  product: windows
detection:
  selection_image:
    Image|endswith: '\osk.exe'
  selection_userpath:
    Image|startswith:
      - 'C:\Users\'
      - 'C:\Windows\Temp\'
      - 'C:\ProgramData\'
  condition: selection_image and selection_userpath
falsepositives:
  - Developer or accessibility tooling that copies the on-screen keyboard next to an application bundle; verify the parent process (the campaign chain starts under dotnet.exe or MSBuild-heavy IDE hosts) and the presence of OskSupport.dll in the same directory
level: high
